///1ED
ED.CMD - Extended help for the CP/M-86 text editor

ED creates and edits plain-text files, including .TXT, .SUB and .A86.
Enter editing commands at its * prompt. Text being edited resides in
a memory buffer, with an internal character pointer called CP.

Start with QUICKSTART or WORKFLOW. COMMANDS provides a reference for
each command letter. EXAMPLES contains exercises; ERRORS explains
common problems.

This help describes Digital Research ED for CP/M-86.
Many commands also exist in CP/M-80, but implementations differ.
Do not use ED to modify binary .CMD files.

Open this help: HELP ED
Look up a command: HELP ED COMMANDS S
Open an exercise: HELP ED EXAMPLES NEWFILE

///2NOTATION
Enter indented command examples at the ED prompt.
Press Return after each command unless instructed otherwise.
A> represents the CP/M prompt. * represents the ED prompt.
Do not type these prompt characters yourself.

^Z means: hold Ctrl and press Z.
^L means: hold Ctrl and press L.
Although this notation uses two visible characters, you must enter
the actual control character when typing an ED command.

Example: sold^Znew^Z
Type s, old, Ctrl-Z, new, Ctrl-Z, then press Return.
Do not type a literal caret followed by the letter Z.

n represents a decimal count; do not type the letter n.
Square brackets in syntax descriptions indicate optional parts.
Use lowercase command letters when you need to preserve mixed-case
text. See CASE.

///2QUICKSTART
Create a new file:
  A>ED TEST.TXT
  -u
  i
Type a few lines, pressing Return after each line.
Press Ctrl-Z to return to command mode.
  b
  #t
  e

Read and edit an existing small file:
  A>ED TEST.TXT
  -u
  #a
  b
  0lt

#A tries to read the remaining source into the buffer. If it does not
fit, ED may report a full buffer. See LARGEFILES.

Save and exit: enter e on a line by itself.
Discard changes since the last save cycle and exit: enter q,
then confirm with Y when prompted.

///2STARTING
Syntax:
  ED [d:]name.ext
  ED [d:]source.ext [e:]target.ext
  ED [d:]source.ext e:

Examples at the CP/M prompt:
  A>ED NOTES.TXT
  A>ED B:PROG.A86
  A>ED SOURCE.TXT NEW.TXT
  A>ED SOURCE.TXT B:

Without a separate destination, the edited result replaces the source
under its original name. A separate destination saves the result
under that name. A destination drive alone uses the same filename
on that drive. Choose a destination filename that does not exist.

NEW FILE means that the specified source does not exist yet.
Even when the source exists, the initial buffer is empty:
you must explicitly read text with A.

Use CP/M 8.3 filenames and select the correct user area.
Start the program as ED, without adding .CMD to its command name.

///2WORKFLOW
ED processes text in this direction:

  unread source -> memory buffer -> temporary output

A reads the next source lines into the buffer.
I inserts new text into the buffer.
W writes the first buffer lines to the output and removes those
lines from the buffer. Output therefore grows from start to finish.
E writes the remaining text and completes the editing operation.

B moves only to the beginning of the current buffer. It does not
rewind the source or retrieve text already written out.
Use H to save and start a new reading cycle.

ED has two input modes:
  Command mode: the * prompt accepts editing commands.
  Insert mode:  characters you type become file contents.

Enter i followed by Return to start inserting.
Press Ctrl-Z to end insertion.
Return on an empty command line also has a meaning: move to the
next text line and display it.

///2POINTER
CP is the internal character pointer: a position between characters.
The visible terminal cursor does not show CP's position in the text.

In these illustrations, | represents CP; it is not part of the file.
Each example starts independently with AB|CDE:
  2d     deletes CD: AB|E
  -d     deletes B:  A|CDE
  ixy^Z  inserts xy: ABxy|CDE

B places CP at the buffer beginning; -B places it at the buffer end.
L moves by lines, C by characters. CR and LF each count as a character.
After a search or insertion, CP normally follows the matched or
inserted text. T may therefore display only the tail of a line.

0LT moves CP to the line beginning and displays the whole line.
0TT displays the whole line without moving CP.
Check your position before using D, K or a substitution command.

///2COUNTS
For most commands, omitting a count means execute once.
  5l     move forward five lines
  -2l    move back two lines
  12c    move forward twelve characters

# represents the maximum count, 65535; it is not a wildcard.
  #t     display all remaining text in the current buffer
  #a     read as many remaining source lines as possible

Negative counts apply only to commands that support backward action.
F, S and N search forward; -F is not a backward-search command.
Use B and search again to find text at an earlier position.

Zero has a different special meaning for each applicable command:
  0a     read until the buffer is approximately half full
  0w     write out approximately half the buffer
  0l     move to the beginning of the current line
  0t     display from the line beginning to CP
  0p     display a page without moving CP
  0v     display free and total buffer space
  0x     delete a transfer file

///2CASE
An uppercase command letter can convert its associated text to
uppercase. This does not make a search case-insensitive.

  fName^Z     searches for exactly Name
  FName^Z     searches for NAME
  iHello^Z    inserts Hello
  IHello^Z    inserts HELLO

U enables persistent uppercase conversion; -U disables it.
Use -u and lowercase command letters when writing ordinary text.
This prevents unintended changes to names and program source.
Search and substitution arguments are also affected.

Examples in this help use lowercase command letters where useful.
HELP topic names may still be entered in uppercase.

///2COMMANDS
Commands by letter:
  A read source      B buffer start/end  C move by characters
  D delete chars     E save and exit     F search buffer
  H save and reopen  I insert text       J replace text region
  K delete lines     L move by lines     M repeat commands
  N search source    O restart edit      P display pages
  Q abandon edit     R insert file       S substitute text
  T display text     U uppercase mode    V numbering/space
  W write output     X export block      Z delay

NUMBERS explains line addresses and ranges.
COMBINING explains command sequences on one line.

///3A
A - Append: read the next source lines at the buffer end.
  a      read one more source line
  20a    read twenty more source lines
  #a     continue until end of source or buffer full
  0a     read until the buffer is approximately half full

A does not reread source lines already processed.
New files have no source lines to read; use I to enter new text.
For a full buffer, see LARGEFILES.
Use lowercase a when uppercase conversion is unwanted.

///3B
B - Beginning/Bottom: move CP within the current buffer.
  b      beginning of buffer
  -b     end of buffer

  b#t    display the entire current buffer from its beginning
  -b     position for inserting new text at the end

B and -B do not change text or read anything from disk.

///3C
C - Character: move CP a specified number of characters.
  c      one character forward
  8c     eight characters forward
  -3c    three characters backward

A CR/LF line separator counts as two characters.
With tabs, a screen column is not the same as a text character.
Prefer L when moving between lines.
Example: b5ct displays the first line from its sixth character.

///3D
D - Delete: remove characters at CP or immediately before it.
  d      delete the next character
  4d     delete four characters to the right of CP
  -d     delete the character immediately to the left of CP
  -4d    delete four characters to the left of CP

D closes the gap; it does not replace deleted text with spaces.
ED has no general UNDO command.
Deleting CR and LF can join two lines together.
See EXAMPLES JOIN for a controlled procedure.

///3E
E - End: finish editing, save the result and exit ED.
  e

ED also copies any unread source text to the output.
You do not need to load the complete file before saving.
E must appear alone on its command line.

When editing under the same filename, the old version normally
becomes name.BAK and the new version receives the original extension.
See FILES for the purpose and limitations of this backup.

///3F
F - Find: search forward from CP within the current buffer.
  ftext^Z
  3ftext^Z

The second form finds the third following occurrence.
After a successful search, CP follows the matched text.
  fname^Z0lt
Finds name and displays the entire line containing it.

Search strings are literal; * and ? are not filename wildcards here.
Certain control characters can also be included in a search string.
If no match is found, ED normally reports BREAK #.
Check your position and the exact case of the text.
F does not load further source sections automatically; use N for that.

///3H
H - Head: save the result and begin editing that result again.
  h

This starts a new save/read cycle without leaving ED.
After H, the buffer is empty. Read the required text again:
  #a
  b

H is useful after W, after a search with N, or for an intermediate save.
H must appear alone on its command line.
O and Q cannot reverse changes already saved by a previous H.
A later save cycle can also replace an older .BAK file.

///3I
I - Insert: add text at the current position.
To enter multiple lines:
  i
Type text, pressing Return after each line.
Press Ctrl-Z to return to command mode.

To insert a short string without entering multiline insert mode:
  iHello^Z

The new text is inserted before the character to the right of CP.
CP then follows the inserted text; existing text is moved along.
Use -u and lowercase i to preserve the case of the text.
Some ED implementations also accept Esc as a terminator.
These examples consistently use Ctrl-Z.

///3J
J - Juxtapose: retain a starting string, insert replacement text,
then delete the old intervening text up to another matching string.
  jstart^Zreplacement^Zend^Z

The starting string and the terminating end string are retained.
Example input:
  NAME=old value;
Command, issued from the beginning of the buffer:
  jNAME=^Znew value^Z;^Z
Result:
  NAME=new value;

Check that both markers exist and identify the intended region.
Use S for a straightforward word or phrase substitution.
A preceding positive count selects a following occurrence of the
starting string. First practise J without a count on a test file.

///3K
K - Kill: delete text through one or more line boundaries.
  k      delete from CP through the current line ending
  3k     delete from CP through three line boundaries
  -k     delete back to the beginning of the preceding line
  0k     delete from the current line beginning to CP

At a line beginning, K removes a complete line.
In the middle of a line, K removes its tail and line separator;
the retained beginning is joined to the next line.

Use 0L first when you want to delete the entire current line:
  0lk
To remove three lines starting at buffer line 5:
  5:3k
There is no general undo. Check the position before deleting.

///3L
L - Line: move to the beginning of another line.
  l      next line
  4l     four lines forward
  -l     preceding line
  -4l    four lines backward
  0l     beginning of the current line

L moves without displaying text; append T to inspect the result.
  0lt    display the whole current line
  -lt    move back one line and display it

///3M
M - Macro: execute a command sequence repeatedly.
  3mcommands

Example, from the buffer beginning with at least three filled lines:
  3m0l3tl
Each repetition displays up to three lines and advances one line.
This shows overlapping sections and does not modify the text.

M without a count repeats until a limit or error is reached.
Always use a small explicit count while learning.

Every text argument inside a macro must have a Ctrl-Z terminator.
Return ends the macro command, so M must be the last component
on its command line. BREAK # can be reported when a macro ends;
inspect the text and the operations that were performed.

///3N
N - Next: search through the source beyond the current buffer.
  ntext^Z

N searches the buffer first. If necessary, ED writes processed text
out, reads another source section and continues searching.
A positive count selects a following numbered occurrence.

N can therefore change the buffer and temporary output.
Use F for a search confined to the current buffer.
After scanning to source end, the buffer can be empty.
Use H to save, reopen and begin another pass from the beginning.
B alone cannot retrieve text that has already been written out.

///3O
O - Original: discard the current editing cycle and start again.
  o

This is the letter O, not the digit zero.
Answer Y to confirm that you want to discard the changes.
ED remains active but starts again with an empty buffer.
Use A to read source text again.

After an earlier H, the source has already been updated.
O cannot restore its contents from before that H.
O is not an undo command for an individual operation.
O must appear alone on its command line.

///3P
P - Page: display text in pages while moving CP.
  p      process and display one page forward
  -p     one page backward
  2p     two pages
  0p     display the current page without moving CP

The classic CP/M-86 documentation describes pages of 23 lines.
This is a fixed editor convention, not modern terminal detection.
Use 0P to inspect a page without movement, or an explicit T count
when you want to control the amount of displayed text.

///3Q
Q - Quit: abandon the current editing cycle without saving it.
  q

Answer Y when ED asks whether to discard the changes.
Answer N to keep editing.
Q must appear alone on its command line.

Changes already saved by H remain saved.
Use E for a normal exit that saves the current changes.
Do not use Ctrl-C as a routine replacement for E or Q.
Depending on context, it interrupts an operation or aborts ED,
without completing normal file handling.

///3R
R - Read: insert another text file at the current position.
  rPART.TXT
  rB:PART.TXT

Specify the extension explicitly if the file is not a .LIB file.
Without an extension, ED assumes .LIB.
Without a filename, ED uses X$$$$$$$.LIB.

The file is inserted before CP. CP remains at the character that
previously followed it, now further along in the buffer.
R does not rewind the source of the current edit.
Make sure the inserted file fits in the buffer.
When combining commands, terminate the filename with Ctrl-Z.

///3S
S - Substitute: search and replace from CP in the current buffer.
  sold^Znew^Z
  3sold^Znew^Z

Without a count, ED replaces the next occurrence.
A count of 3 requests three successive substitutions.
  bsred^Zblue^Z0lt
Searches from the buffer beginning, replaces once and shows the line.

To replace all remaining occurrences in the current buffer:
  b#sold^Znew^Z
When no further match exists, BREAK # can be reported.
Earlier successful replacements are not reversed by this message.
S does not automatically process unread source outside the buffer.

An empty replacement string deletes the matched text:
  sold^Z^Z
Case matters: lowercase s preserves the specified case when
persistent uppercase conversion has been disabled with -U.

///3T
T - Type: display text without moving CP.
  t      display from CP to the current line ending
  5t     display five lines starting at CP
  -3t    display text extending three lines back from CP
  #t     display everything from CP to the buffer end
  0t     display from the current line beginning to CP
  0tt    display the entire current line, preserving CP

B#T displays the complete buffer. It reads no further source lines.
0LT also displays a whole line but moves CP to the line beginning.
For manageable output, use smaller sections such as 10T and 10L.

///3U
U - Upper case: enable or disable persistent uppercase conversion.
  u      enable conversion
  -u     disable conversion

This controls subsequent text input and associated processing.
It does not convert the entire existing file in one operation.
The case of the command letter can independently enable conversion.
Use -u together with lowercase i, f and s for mixed-case text.

///3V
V - Verify: control line numbering or inspect buffer space.
  v      display line numbers
  -v     hide line numbers
  0v     display free and total buffer space

The values displayed by 0V are byte counts, not line counts.
Line numbers appear only on the screen, not in the text file.
Adding or deleting lines changes subsequent line numbers.
Numbers refer to the current buffer. They are not permanent line
addresses for the complete disk file when processing it in sections.

///3W
W - Write: write the first buffer lines to the temporary output.
  10w    write the first ten lines and free their buffer space
  0w     write out approximately half the buffer
  #w     write out the entire buffer

W starts at the buffer beginning, not at CP.
Use X to export a block starting at CP to a separate file.
W does not complete editing; eventually finish with E or H.

Text already written out is no longer accessible with B or -L.
H saves the complete cycle so you can read the text again.
Buffer line numbers change after writing lines out.

///3X
X - Xfer: copy a text block from CP to a separate file.
  4xBLOCK.LIB
  4x

The second form uses the default filename X$$$$$$$.LIB.
Without an extension, ED assumes .LIB.
X neither removes the text from the buffer nor moves CP.
A later X to the same file appends text to that file.

Delete an old transfer file first if you want a fresh block:
  0xBLOCK.LIB
This deletes the disk file; it is not an empty copy operation.
Do not use it on a file you want to retain.

After X, use R to insert the block elsewhere.
To move a block, use K to delete the original lines after X.
See BLOCKS for a complete procedure.

///3Z
Z - Delay: pause execution, for example inside a macro.
  2z

This does not modify text. Exact timing depends on the ED version,
processor and emulator. Do not use it as an accurate clock.
The command letter Z is different from the control character Ctrl-Z.

///2NUMBERS
Enable line numbers with V.
  12:       move CP to the beginning of buffer line 12
  12:t      display buffer line 12
  3::7t     display lines 3 through 7, inclusive
  3::7k     delete lines 3 through 7, inclusive

n: selects a line. :n before T, L or K specifies the final line
through which the command should operate. Both forms can be combined.

Notice the difference:
  10        move ten lines forward and display the destination line
  10:       move to buffer line 10
  10t       display ten lines from CP without moving CP

Editing and W can change the line numbers.
Inspect the relevant lines again before deleting a range.

///2COMBINING
ED executes commands on the same line from left to right.
  b5t       move to the beginning and display five lines
  0l3d0lt   move to line start, delete three characters, show result
  fword^Z0lt

A text argument needs an actual Ctrl-Z terminator if another command
follows it. Without a separator, that command letter can become part
of the text argument instead.

For the final text on a normal command line, ED also accepts Return
as the terminator. These exercises use explicit Ctrl-Z terminators
so the meaning remains clear if more commands are added later.
Inside a macro, every text argument must be properly terminated.

E, H, O and Q must each appear alone on their command line.
Keep command lines short. Classic CP/M-86 limits are 128 characters,
or 100 characters when a command line contains text arguments.

///2BLOCKS
Copy a block:
  0xWORK.LIB
  3:
  2xWORK.LIB
  10:
  rWORK.LIB

This copies original lines 3 and 4 to a position before line 10.
Check that WORK.LIB contains only disposable material before deleting
it. Otherwise X would append the block to an existing transfer file.

Move a block:
  0xWORK.LIB
  3:
  2xWORK.LIB
  2k
  b#t

Now select the destination using the displayed text.
Line numbers changed when K removed the original lines.
At the chosen destination, execute rWORK.LIB.
Check the result before deleting the transfer file.
Save the completed edit with E or H.

///2LARGEFILES
A large file does not have to fit entirely in the buffer.
Process it in sections from beginning to end:
  0a
  b
  10t
Edit the loaded lines, then write processed text out:
  0w
  0a

Repeat as required while the remaining source exceeds buffer space.
0W may remove only part of the buffer. Retained text stays in memory,
and further source lines are appended after it.
Do not write out text you still need to edit in the current cycle.

0V displays available space.
A full-buffer message after #A does not mean nothing was read:
a substantial section may already be loaded. Use B to edit its start.
Write processed lines out with W to make room for more source text.

E includes the remaining buffer and the unread source in its output.
H saves and starts another cycle so earlier sections can be reached
by reading them into the buffer again.

///2FILES
When editing under the same name, ED creates temporary output with
the .$$$ extension. After successful completion, the old source
becomes a .BAK file and the new text receives the original name.

Example after editing ED LETTER.TXT and saving:
  LETTER.TXT    the new result
  LETTER.BAK    the preceding source version

.BAK is one backup version, not a history of every edit.
A later save cycle can replace the previous .BAK.
Do not edit a backup under its .BAK extension; first copy or rename
it to another working filename.

With a separate destination filename, the original source is retained.
For important exercises, for example, use:
  A>ED SOURCE.TXT TEST.TXT

W is not a complete save cycle. E or H must complete file handling.
Do not delete temporary .$$$ files while ED is using them.

///2KEYS
Useful corrections while entering a command:
  Return    execute command; on an empty line, show the next line
  Ctrl-H    delete the last character typed on the input line
  Ctrl-U    cancel the current input line
  Ctrl-X    cancel the current input line
  DEL/RUB   delete the last character; display behaviour may vary
  Ctrl-E    start a physical screen line without ending the command

In insert mode, Return ends a real text line.
Ctrl-Z ends insert mode or terminates a text argument.

Arrow keys, Home, End and function keys are not standard ED navigation.
Even on a VT100 terminal, use B, C, L, T and P to navigate.
Escape sequences from special keys can otherwise become input text.

Use E or Q for normal exit. Ctrl-C behaves according to context:
it can interrupt output, a macro or the editor itself.
Do not treat Ctrl-C as a save command.

///2EXAMPLES
These exercises use separate practice text files.
^Z always means an actual Ctrl-Z keystroke.
Press Return after each ED command.
While entering text, press Return after each text line.

Exercises: NEWFILE, CHANGE, INSERT, DELETE, JOIN and SPLIT.

///3NEWFILE
Create a small file:
  A>ED TEST.TXT
  -u
  i
Enter these three text lines:
  One red bicycle.
  Two green trees.
  Three blue doors.
Press Ctrl-Z at the start of the next empty input line.
  b#t

Check the three lines and save:
  e

Then check the file at the CP/M prompt:
  A>TYPE TEST.TXT

///3CHANGE
Open the file created in NEWFILE and replace red with yellow:
  A>ED TEST.TXT
  -u
  #a
  b
  sred^Zyellow^Z
  0lt

The first line should now read:
  One yellow bicycle.

Inspect the whole buffer with B#T. Save with E if the result is correct.
To abandon this exercise, use Q and answer Y.

///3INSERT
Insert a new line before the second buffer line:
  2:
  i
Type:
  An extra line.
Press Return, then Ctrl-Z.
  b#t

To append new text, use -B followed by i and Return.
To insert words at the beginning of a line, for example:
  2:iPlease note: ^Z
The space before Ctrl-Z is part of the inserted text.

///3DELETE
First display the intended range:
  2::3t
Then delete those two lines:
  2:2k
Inspect the result:
  b#t

To delete only the first four characters of buffer line 1:
  b4d0lt

These are separate examples. If executed consecutively, the second
operation acts on the result of the first.
Use Q if you do not want to save the exercise.

///3JOIN
Start with these two normal CR/LF lines:
  This is
  an example.

Go to line 1, then line 2, then back two characters.
CP is now immediately before the first line's CR/LF:
  1:l-2c
Delete the two separator characters and insert a space:
  2di ^Z
Display the result:
  0lt

Expected text:
  This is an example.

This procedure assumes normal CR/LF line endings.
Do not apply it to a final line that lacks a normal line separator.

///3SPLIT
Start with:
  One line with two parts.

Go to the buffer beginning and search through the word 'with':
  bfwith^Z
Delete the following space:
  d
Enter insert mode at that position:
  i
Press Return to insert a line separator.
Press Ctrl-Z to leave insert mode.
  b#t

Expected text:
  One line with
  two parts.

///2ERRORS
BREAK # during F or S: no further matching text in the buffer.
Check case, search text and CP; use B to start again if appropriate.
With a large repetition count, earlier substitutions may already
have succeeded. Inspect the text before repeating the command.

BREAK >: buffer full or a text argument too long.
Check 0V, process the buffer in sections or shorten the command.
BREAK ? with a letter: invalid command or command combination.
Check that E, H, O or Q really is alone on its command line.

BREAK O during R: the requested library file was not found.
Check drive, user area, filename and extension.
BREAK E can indicate interrupted command execution.
BREAK F with DISK FULL or DIRECTORY FULL: output cannot continue.

When disk space runs out, do not simply exit with Q.
If necessary, export current buffer text to another writable drive,
for example with B#XB:RESCUE.TXT.
This saves only the current buffer, not automatically text already
written with W or unread source. Also check the temporary .$$$ file.

///2TROUBLE
No text appears after starting ED.
  An existing source is not yet in the buffer. Use A to read it.

T displays only part of my line.
  CP is in the middle of the line. Use 0LT or 0TT.

My text unexpectedly becomes UPPERCASE.
  Use -u and lowercase i, f or s. See CASE.

B does not return to the beginning of the whole file.
  B works only within the buffer. After W or N, earlier text may
  already be in temporary output. Use H to start a new reading cycle.

A substitution happens only once.
  S without a count replaces once. Use 3S or #S within the buffer.

Arrow keys produce strange characters.
  ED uses its own line and character commands. See KEYS.

Changes disappeared after Q.
  Q discards the current cycle. Use E to save and exit.

///2REFERENCE
  A nA #A 0A       read source text
  B -B             buffer beginning / buffer end
  nC -nC           move characters forward / backward
  nD -nD           delete characters
  E                save and exit
  Ftext^Z          search within buffer
  H                save and start again
  I or Itext^Z     insert lines or text
  Jstart^Znew^Zend^Z   replace an intervening text region
  nK -nK 0K        delete through line boundaries
  nL -nL 0L        move to line beginnings
  nMcommands       repeat commands
  Ntext^Z          search with further source reading
  O                discard current cycle and restart
  nP -nP 0P        display pages
  Q                discard current cycle and exit
  Rfile            insert a text file
  nSold^Znew^Z     substitute text
  nT -nT 0T        display text without moving CP
  U -U             persistent uppercase conversion on / off
  V -V 0V          numbering on / off; buffer statistics
  nW 0W #W         write the first buffer lines out
  nXfile           copy a block from CP to a separate file
  0Xfile           delete that separate file
  nZ               delay
  n:               move to buffer line n
  :nT :nL :nK      operate through buffer line n
  n or -n          move lines and display the destination line

Use lowercase command letters to preserve case in text arguments.
^Z is an actual Ctrl-Z control character, not a literal caret and Z.

///2SOURCES
An independent English explanation with original practice examples.
Commands follow Digital Research ED for CP/M-86.

Reference used to check syntax and version differences:
Digital Research, CP/M-86 Operating System User's Guide,
second printing, July 1982: section 4.7 and chapter 5.

Online scan:
https://bitsavers.org/pdf/digitalResearch/cpm-86/
CPM-86_Users_Guide_Jul82.pdf
Join the preceding two URL lines without spaces.

CP/M-80, CP/M Plus and vendor-specific implementations can differ.
This help does not claim testing against your particular ED.CMD.

File format: ///1 root topic, ///2 topic, ///3 subtopic.
ASCII, CRLF and Ctrl-Z padding for the HELP.CMD format discussed here.
